<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  ai.hendricks.visibility-weekly

  NOT INSTALLED. This file is a proposal, version-controlled with the system it
  would run. It does nothing until Brandon copies and loads it. Installing a
  recurring job that spends money on his machine is his decision, not an
  agent's.

  To install:
    cp /Users/brandonlhendricks/dev/hendricks-ai/.claude/state/ai.hendricks.visibility-weekly.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ai.hendricks.visibility-weekly.plist

  To remove:
    launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ai.hendricks.visibility-weekly.plist
    rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ai.hendricks.visibility-weekly.plist

  To test before trusting it:
    /Users/brandonlhendricks/dev/hendricks-ai/scripts/visibility-run.sh --dry-run

  RUNS ON THE MACBOOK, not the Ultra. The MacBook is the head: the repo, the
  state file and the reports live here. The script reaches the Ultra over ssh
  to run the probe, so the Ultra needs to be up and Tailscale needs to be
  current. Tailscale key expiry is the recurring failure mode and it surfaces
  as exit 1 with "cannot reach ultra over ssh" in the error log.

  WEEKLY, MONDAY 09:15. The reasoning is in docs/20-AGENTIC-SYSTEM.md section 6.
  In short: whether an engine cites anyone at all was stable across 51 of 51
  cells in a same-day repeat, so daily probing does not detect state changes any
  sooner. Which sources it cites churns at a mean overlap of 0.68 with nothing
  changed, so a daily source-set delta is indistinguishable from noise, and a
  Class B candidate needs two consecutive runs to confirm no matter how often
  the probe runs. Weekly is the fastest cadence that produces a confirmable
  finding, and it costs about $0.38 a week.

  09:15 rather than overnight, for two reasons. The shared 06:00 job on the
  Ultra runs all thirteen clients and must be clear first. And a laptop is more
  likely to be awake mid-morning than at 03:00, so a failure is seen the same
  day rather than discovered a week later. If the machine is asleep at 09:15,
  launchd runs the job at the next wake, which is the correct behaviour here:
  a late measurement is worth more than a skipped one.

  Cost: about $0.38 per run, roughly $20 a year. The script refuses to spend
  if the dry-run estimate exceeds $2.00.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>ai.hendricks.visibility-weekly</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/brandonlhendricks/dev/hendricks-ai/scripts/visibility-run.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/brandonlhendricks/dev/hendricks-ai</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/brandonlhendricks</string>
    </dict>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Weekday</key>
        <integer>1</integer>
        <key>Hour</key>
        <integer>9</integer>
        <key>Minute</key>
        <integer>15</integer>
    </dict>

    <!-- Never at load. Loading the job must not spend money as a side effect. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <!-- One cycle per fire. The script also refuses a second cycle on a day
         that already has one, so a double fire costs nothing. -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>3600</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/brandonlhendricks/Library/Logs/hendricks-visibility/launchd-visibility-weekly.out</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/brandonlhendricks/Library/Logs/hendricks-visibility/launchd-visibility-weekly.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
